```

This version waits for the fetch to finish before using the data, and it checks whether the request succeeded before trying to read the response.

## Cause

The problem is `this`. Inside a regular `function`, `this` does not point to the `Timer` object. Instead, when `setInterval` calls the function, `this` points to the global object (or is `undefined` in strict mode).

So `this.seconds` is not the `seconds` property on your `Timer` instance. It's `undefined`, and `undefined + 1` gives `NaN`.

## Fix

Use an arrow function instead. Arrow functions do not have their own `this` — they use `this` from the surrounding code, which in this case is the `Timer` instance.

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```

Now `this` correctly refers to the `Timer` instance, and `this.seconds` increases as expected.

